She scooped the river’s silvery mud and pressed it into a jar so smooth the surface reflected a second sky. When the jar dried, she found a single finger-wreath of green glass twined around its lip—an omen, the hawk said, that every truth has a color.

That night, the river woke in a dream, whispering secrets to the jar: the names of the drowned fishermen, the moments a mother chose hope over fear, the treaty that saved a village from famine. Lira, in awe, poured the river’s memory into the jar until the vessel hummed with a soft, sorrowful music.

Morning came. The jar stood on the clay table, not spilling, not fading. People came to drink from it and learned to listen to what the river remembered. The village learned mercy, and the river learned to forgive.
